  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Try a squared difference inside each root",
      "body": "<p>Compare each three-term expression with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Use the first and last terms to suggest a and b, then check the middle term.</p>",
      "recap": "The cross term checks whether a proposed squared difference is correct.",
      "pitfall": "A square root cannot be split across an addition or subtraction.",
      "pause": "Rewrite both expressions as squares.",
      "recall": [],
      "recallOmissionReason": "This is the elementary expansion of a squared difference used to recognise a surd square. The body states the identity. No catalogue lesson is falsely cited as specifically teaching this recognition step."
    },
    {
      "title": "Check signs before taking the roots",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. Compare the two positive parts of each difference to decide its sign.</p>",
      "recap": "A principal square root is nonnegative.",
      "pitfall": "Do not remove an absolute value before checking the sign.",
      "pause": "Simplify each root and add the results.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l01",
          "reminder": "A principal square root is nonnegative."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The first radicand satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, while the second satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>For real z, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, because both terms are positive and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Thus the roots are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> respectively.</p><p>Their sum is <strong>2, option E.</strong> The signs are essential: the negative of a displayed square base has the same square but is not the principal root.</p>",
